1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,521
Look up on a clear day.

2
00:00:02,171 --> 00:00:04,877
The sky is blue — but sunlight is white.

3
00:00:04,527 --> 00:00:06,710
So where does the blue come from?

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,203
Sunlight is every colour travelling together.

5
00:00:12,853 --> 00:00:15,423
Each colour is a wave: red with long,

6
00:00:15,073 --> 00:00:17,890
lazy wiggles, blue with short, quick ones.

7
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,076
To reach your eyes, it has to cross the air:

8
00:00:23,726 --> 00:00:28,960
trillions of nitrogen and oxygen molecules, far smaller than any wave.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:36,520
When a wave meets a molecule, it gets bounced off in a new direction.

10
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:37,474
That's scattering.

11
00:00:37,124 --> 00:00:40,336
And short waves scatter much more than long ones.

12
00:00:39,986 --> 00:00:42,960
Blue scatters about five times more than red.

13
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,530
So blue gets bounced all across the sky,

14
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:54,141
and from every direction some of it heads to you.

15
00:00:53,791 --> 00:00:55,947
Violet scatters even more,

16
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:59,300
but sunlight has less of it, and our eyes favour blue.

17
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,966
At sunset, light skims through far more air.

18
00:01:05,616 --> 00:01:09,906
Most of the blue is scattered away before it arrives,

19
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:11,630
leaving reds and oranges.

20
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,627
Short waves scatter most.

21
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:21,540
That's why the sky is blue by day, and glows red at sunset.

